content.text:
The legacy-wal test: after maintenance retry succeeds, the queued advisory message still contains "transfer is pending" — the test expects the advisory to be REPLACED with the final message. What replaced it before? The `reconcilePending` re-drive → the failure-path publishAdvisory... Previously the failure-path publishAdvisory ran UNCONDITIONALLY on every delivery — the replay re-published with legacyTransferPending now false → replacePendingBackgroundAdvisory replaced the stale message. NOW the parent_advisory step was already marked completed on the first delivery → the replay's runCloseoutStep skips → the stale message stays.

Hmm. This is a real behavioral regression: the advisory correction path relied on re-publishing. The stale message says "transfer is pending; durable reconciliation will retry" — after the retry succeeded, the message is outdated. The test asserts maintenance replaces it.

Options:
(a) In the failure path, don't mark parent_advisory completed when legacyTransferPending — keep it pending until the transfer completes, so the later replay re-publishes the corrected message. That matches the semantics: the advisory content depends on the transfer state; only mark completed when the message is final (transfer done or manual recovery). But careful — infinite pending if transfer never resolves? The transfer pending is reconciled by maintenance until it resolves (retry_pending) — the journal step stays pending, and each maintenance re-drive re-runs publishAdvisory (dedup by eventId... putPendingBackgroundAdvisory with same eventId but DIFFERENT message returns null → no update! The replacement path uses replacePendingBackgroundAdvisory inside publishAdvisory when message lacks the marker... wait: publishAdvisory's needsCorrection = !message.includes(LEGACY_MARKER). The pending-transfer message INCLUDES the marker → needsCorrection false → putPendingBackgroundAdvisory → different message than stored? If stored message has marker and new message has marker (same?) → if identical → returns existing. Once the transfer resolves, the re-driven publish produces a message WITHOUT the marker → needsCorrection true → replacePendingBackgroundAdvisory(correlationId, input) → replaces. 

So (a): in the coder failure path (and success path with legacyTransferPending), when legacyTransferPending is true, run the publish effect but DON'T mark parent_advisory completed — the step stays pending; the legacy reconciler's later reconcilePending re-drive re-publishes with the final message and THEN marks completed. But wait — reconcilePending re-drive: does the failure path even run on reconcilePending? Yes — event() with trustedTerminal → failure arm → parent advisory step pending → publish (with transfer now resolved → corrected message) → mark completed.

But there's a wrinkle: on the FIRST delivery, if I never mark, then every duplicate delivery re-publishes — putPendingBackgroundAdvisory dedups identical messages (same eventId+message → returns existing, no new write). OK.

So the fix: in the coder failure path (and the success-path final advisory), only mark parent_advisory completed when !legacyTransferPending (i.e., the message is final). Actually also legacyTransferRequiresManualRecovery — that IS final (message says manual recovery needed) — mark completed.

Implement: modify the failure-path runCloseoutStep for parent_advisory: add a condition — if legacyTransferPending, publish but skip the mark. runCloseoutStep always marks... I need a variant. Let me add an optional `markCompleted` boolean param to runCloseoutStep, or handle at call site: 

```ts
if (legacyTransferPending) {
  // publish without acking — the message is not final until the transfer resolves
  if (isTerminalCloseoutStepPending(record, terminal.eventId, 'parent_advisory')) {
    await publishAdvisory(...);  // no mark
  }
} else {
  await runCloseoutStep(...);
}
```

Same for the success-path final advisory (coder 'completed and settled; transfer pending...'). And the ingestion-failure arm's parent_advisory (legacyTransferPending variant there too).

Also the non-coder duplicate early-return arm: no legacy transfer for non-coders — fine.

Now check the OTHER two failing files: legacy-wal-advisory-replacement (likely same root cause) and async-lifecycle-integration-matrix. Let me look at the matrix failure after fixing this.

